<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1938 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Erika</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German marching song of 1938 about a flower and a sweetheart, sung by Hitler's army in the Second World War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Herms Niel</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Herms Niel</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Wehrmacht and the Reich Labour Service of Nazi Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany (published in Großburgwedel, near Hanover)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1938</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music by Herms Niel, published 1938)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Erika&quot; is a German marching song with words and music by Herms Niel, published in 1938 by the firm of Louis Oertel in Großburgwedel, a village north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. Its date is often given as &quot;about 1930&quot;, but this has never been substantiated; in an interview with the Viennese newspaper Das Kleine Volksblatt, Niel said he was suddenly inspired to write it in the summer of 1938 after a comic mishap on a walk, when his dog ran into a wasps' nest. Erika is both a German woman's name and the German word for heather.</p><p>Niel joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in early May 1933 and rose under the regime to be leading bandmaster of the music corps of the Reich Labour Service. He wrote many marching songs that largely served Nazi propaganda. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>, the propaganda minister, valued simple popular songs as a tool, and between 1933 and 1945 some 15,000 Nazi musical works were produced, spread through the new mass media of film and radio.</p><p>The song was soon taken up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> as a soldiers' song, and according to the British soldier and historian Major General Michael Tillotson it was the most popular marching song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. After every line, and after each time the name is sung, a three-beat pause is filled by the bass drum or, without a band, by the stamp of marching feet; the German article notes that the sound recalls gunfire and gives the song its character as a war song. It belonged to the run of popular songs with women's names in which soldiers sang of the wives and sweethearts at home, and its picture of the faithful, waiting woman matched the role the Nazi regime promoted for women; soldiers also sang ruder versions. The writer Gregor von Rezzori recalled it on German radio in April 1945 among the reports of victorious &quot;defensive battles&quot;, and it appears in many novels about the Nazi period, among them Walter Kempowski's Heile Welt.</p><p>Abroad the song has been heard as typically German but remains closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>: in 1983, at the tenth anniversary of the junta in Chile, a Chilean army band in field grey and Wehrmacht-style helmets played it, and Andrei Eshpai quoted it in his Symphony No. 5 (1985), perhaps to depict the Wehrmacht's advance. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> it stayed popular after the war, detached from its origin: it was sung on marches of the Bundeswehr and the Austrian army after rearmament, recorded as a folk-style song, for instance by Tony Marshall in 1974, and is regarded by some as a folk song. It is used in films about the war, among them Steven Spielberg's Schindler's List (1993), and there are versions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, best known by the tenor Gé Korsten, and in Finnish as &quot;Kaarina&quot;.</p><p>In recent years its use by right-wing groups has drawn condemnation. In June 2025 members of the University of Warwick Conservative Association sang and danced to it at an event, which university representatives condemned; in December 2025 two representatives of a conservative student party at the Vrije Universiteit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> were arrested over an assault on a student who, witnesses said, had criticised them for singing it; and on 6 September 2026 the Alternative for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s official YouTube channel played a remix of it during its broadcast on the night of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> state election.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A marching song about blooming heather and a sweetheart waiting at home, its pauses filled by drumbeats or marching feet; brisk and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="3990095" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="8051179" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Herms-niel-erika-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-herms-niel-erika-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 5 (Andrei Eshpai)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1985</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hinaus in die Ferne (Tony Marshall)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Erika (Afrikaans version, sung by Gé Korsten)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kaarina (Finnish version)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Schindler's List (Steven Spielberg)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1993</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li></ul></section>
